source=json.loads((D/'source.json').read_text());questions={x[':question/math-academy-id']:x for x in source['record'][':knowledge-point/questions']}
classify=['# Classification','Provenance: math, basis 1356, lineage 01a12a15-3e0a-75a1-9dac-f8b8c5d5c3f1. Ten practice questions: seven easy, one moderate, one hard, one unrated. All use one `selection` radio field with five scalar mathematical values. The canonical example is not counted as practice.','| Family | Source | Observed level | Plot rating | Stage distinction |','|---|---|---|---|---|']
stages={'log_square':'Reciprocal derivative, positive-domain perfect square, logarithmic antiderivative; source solution unavailable','product_trig':'Product-rule expansion and cancellation, Pythagorean identity, positive t, quadratic endpoint difference','poly_negative':'Polynomial perfect square, negative lower bound; explicit substitution, sign distribution, evaluation','quadratic_two':'Two nonzero linear velocities, nonnegative t, radical coefficient; direct final evaluation','poly_ordered':'Three nonzero polynomial velocities, quartic perfect square; component evaluation then collection','chain_product':'Product and chain-rule cancellations at frequency 2; Pythagorean identity and nonnegative t; direct final evaluation','poly_constant':'Constant component, expanded quartic sum then perfect square; component evaluation then collection','helix':'Linear plus sine/cosine, constant speed; factor-out integration row and endpoint difference','quadratic_three':'Three nonzero linear velocities, perfect-square coefficient norm; direct final evaluation','poly_radical':'Radical polynomial derivative, quartic perfect square; component evaluation then collection'}
for name,c in g.TEMPLATES.items():classify.append('| '+name+' | '+c['source_id']+' | '+('unrated (authored moderate)' if name=='log_square' else c['level'])+' | '+('moderate' if name=='log_square' else 'none')+' | '+stages[name]+' |')
classify+=['Each family has one complete observed bank profile. Numeric coefficients and component order differ materially across the seven easy families, so their derivative and simplification masks remain separate. The two product families differ in the displayed product-rule row, chain-rule workload, and evaluation row count.','Lesson order, reconstructed from activity/first-step and step/next: Introduction → A Concrete Example → integral-expression KP19743 → planar-evaluation KP19744 → spatial-evaluation KP19860 → Deriving the Arc Length Formula. Prerequisites are differentiating vector-valued functions, Pythagorean identity, and planar parametric arc length. Next topics are scalar line integrals and parameterization by arc length; neither is included.','All observed difficulty levels are implemented. The unrated log-square question receives an explicitly authored moderate label: it uses reciprocal/logarithmic differentiation and a positive-domain perfect square, followed by a familiar logarithmic antiderivative. No numerical quadrature, inverse hyperbolic antiderivatives, signed-domain branch splitting, or new integration skill is introduced.']
(D/'classification.md').write_text('\n\n'.join(classify)+'\n')
extract=['# Four-stage template extraction','## 1. Specific source descriptions','The following records describe every source independently; exact prompts, fields, original choice values/IDs and solutions are preserved in source.json.']
for name,c in g.TEMPLATES.items():
 q=questions[c['source_id']];extract+=['### '+c['source_id']+' — '+name,q[':question/problem'],'Coordinates: '+', '.join('`'+x+'`' for x in c['curve'])+'. Interval ['+str(c['a'])+','+str(c['b'])+']. Speed `'+c['speed']+'`. '+stages[name]+'. Numeric endpoint-stage count: '+str(c['numeric_rows'])+'.', 'Original field shape: radio selection, five complete scalar exact expressions; no distractor-specific feedback.']
extract+=['## 2. General mathematical pattern','For a displayed three-coordinate curve, differentiate each component, compute the Euclidean magnitude, simplify using a perfect-square polynomial or the Pythagorean identity with the displayed interval domain, and integrate the speed exactly between the displayed endpoints. Each source family retains its own intermediate stage masks and row count. Intervals are parameterized; fixed curves are mathematical components of these templates, not literal whole-question replay branches.','## 3. Components and complete bank mapping','The choices below are matched as whole expressions. For all non-logarithmic observed banks, scalar values underdetermine original error operations. The replay-only amplitude mapping is explicitly `v_i(a,b) = ratio_i * L(a,b)`; ratios describe the observed numerical shape, not recovered author intent. These profiles are never defaults. This is evidence preservation, not a claim that proportional perturbation teaches an error recipe. The separately authored default is a complete operation bank: correct L; half speed integral L/2; doubled speed integral 2L; omission of the norm square root ∫v²; omission of integration v(b). All five must be positive, real, unequal, and have exactly one correct value. Failure rejects the whole parameter instance.']
maprows=[]
for name,c in g.TEMPLATES.items():
 q=questions[c['source_id']];a,b=g.expr(str(c['a'])),g.expr(str(c['b']));L=g.math_data(name,a,b)[3];choices=q[':question/answer-fields'][0][':answer-field/choices'];correct=q[':question/answer-fields'][0].get(':answer-field/correct')
 extract+=['### observed_'+c['source_id'],'| Source answer UUID | Original value | Parameterized shape | Correct status |','|---|---|---|---|']
 log_shapes=['1/b+b²−2a²','b²−a²','b²+a²','1/b²+b²−2a²','ln(b/a)+b²−a²']
 for i,ch in enumerate(choices):
  v=g.parse_tex(ch[':answer/value']);ratio=sp.simplify(v/L);recipe=log_shapes[i] if name=='log_square' else 'L × ('+str(ratio)+')'
  status=('independently derived correct; original designation missing' if v==L else 'wrong') if correct is None else ('correct' if ch[':answer/id']==correct[':answer/id'] else 'wrong')
  extract.append('| '+ch[':answer/id']+' | $'+ch[':answer/value']+'$ | `'+recipe+'` | '+status+' |')
  maprows.append({'source_id':c['source_id'],'field_key':'selection','bank_profile':'observed_'+c['source_id'],'answer_id':ch[':answer/id'],'source_value':ch[':answer/value'],'recipe':recipe,'interpretation':'observed expression shape; original error intent unresolved' if status=='wrong' else status,'reproduces_original_exact_value':True})
extract+=['## 4. Reusable masks, constraints and calculations','templates.json stores prompt bound slots, complete source derivative/norm/prose masks and ordered endpoint row slots. It preserves whether an intermediate substitution row appears, whether evaluated terms are displayed before collection, and whether the source evaluates directly. No short-expression substitution is applied across coordinates, function names, exponent values or prose. The builder replaces only entire endpoint components; exponent powers remain fixed. Answers use exact SymPy integration, with no floating-point correctness test.','Bounds for polynomial/helix easy templates: half-integers from −3 through 5, increasing with span ≤5; all four speed-proportional-to-t families require a≥0. The moderate product-trig notice uses t≥0 when the lower endpoint is zero and retains t>0 for a positive lower endpoint. Moderate product-trig and hard chain-product bounds: half-integers 0 through 5 with a<b. Log-square bounds: increasing pairs from {1/2,1,2,e}. Explicit parameters enforce the same guards and are never silently resampled. These endpoint policies are authored sampling bounds, not observed source code. Interval endpoints materially change assessed integration/evaluation; constant coordinate values remain fixed and do not inflate variety counts. Mathematical fingerprints identify canonical speed and assessed interval (constant coordinates and translations do not affect the assessed operation), excluding profiles/banks/order. Content identities additionally include version, prompt, fixed solution and full bank (plot asset-path normalization is reported separately); option order/seed do not create identities.','Source anomalies and supported repairs: q-127635 and q-127633 each have an extra closing parenthesis in the first derivative tuple; q-76908 is missing a comma between derivative components in that first row. These three are presentation repairs; all original mathematics remains in evidence. Six available solutions match exactly after documented whitespace/redundant-group notation normalization; all nine match after the three presentation repairs. The q-33759 source lacks difficulty, correct ref and worked solution. Its source choice bank and prompt are preserved; correct designation and solution are explicitly authored. Independent derivation: r′=(1/t,2,2t), v²=1/t²+4+4t²=(1/t+2t)²; t>0 implies v=1/t+2t. Thus L=[ln t+t²]₁ᵉ=(1+e²)−1=e². There are nine observed correct-designation matches, not ten.','Authored defaults remain within each observed level’s method and workload. The seven easy families are unordered alternatives, moderate retains the observed explicit product-rule simplification (plus the separately assigned unrated log family), and hard retains both product/chain rules at frequency 2. Changing endpoints adds no new skill. All banks remain positive arc-length quantities; squared-speed choices can be numerically larger but are real and physically admissible lengths. No diagnosis prose has been invented.']
(D/'template-extraction.md').write_text('\n\n'.join(extract)+'\n');(D/'bank-mapping.json').write_text(json.dumps(maprows,ensure_ascii=False,indent=2)+'\n')
(D/'plot-extraction.md').write_text('''# Plot extraction and specification

1. Specific inspection: the practice q-33759 plot is a white-background three-dimensional coordinate sketch. Red curve context continues before t=1 and after t=e; blue marks the assessed segment. Blue arrows show increasing t. Endpoint labels t=1 and t=e and dashed coordinate projections label y=2, y=2e, z=1, z=e². The logarithmic x coordinate increases monotonically. The canonical helix similarly uses red context and a blue segment from 0 to 2π with arrows. Both original PNGs were opened visually; only the practice plot requires regeneration.

2. General pattern: a spatial curve sketch indicates the interval segment and increasing parameter direction. It illustrates the path; the analytic prompt carries all assessed information.

3. Components: r(t)=(ln t,2t,t²); blue assessed segment on [a,b]; red context on [max(.08,.65a),a] and [b,1.15b]; endpoint markers/labels derive from a,b; dashed projections derive from exact endpoint coordinates; three interior tangent arrows derive from (1/t,2,2t). Axes x/y/z, a consistent perspective, white exported background and readable parameter labels are fixed. There are no plotted choice or worked-solution options.

4. Reusable renderer: render_plot.py uses Matplotlib’s 3D axes and cached Python3.10 dependencies read-only when the primary Python3.13 environment lacks Matplotlib. No dependencies are installed. Each invocation receives a unique temporary JSON file, writes a unique temporary PNG and atomically replaces its content-keyed final path. Fixed mathematical data determine output. Every interval, including the original, uses the same renderer; raw source images remain inspection evidence only. Bounds derive from context curve points; no independent sampling occurs. The renderer preserves mathematical geometry, assessed/context coloring, arrows, endpoint labels and projection relationships while using native Matplotlib perspective rather than imitating the hand-drawn source pixel style. Plot rating is moderate for the log family, none for the other nine families. Plot fingerprints include parameterization and assessed interval: traversal endpoints are assessed.

Visual QA: curve-dd81d46cabee77dd6056.png (original interval [1,e]) and curve-199000cc8e9898ba6f5b.png ([1/2,2]) were opened. Blue segments and endpoint coordinates match; red extensions, increasing-t blue arrows, black origin axes/arrowheads, dashed endpoint projections and exact coordinate labels are present. Native Matplotlib perspective differs from the hand-drawn source; semantic mathematical cues are retained. The canonical example image remains raw inspection evidence only.
''')
